{
    "background_story": "Poppy, a beautiful and charming young woman, lived in 19th-century Europe. She caught the eye of the dashing Prince Henry, who was immediately smitten. Despite their deep affection for each other, their social differences made a traditional marriage impossible. Poppy was a commoner, and the prince's family would never approve of their union. Undeterred, the prince decided to marry Poppy in a morganatic marriage, a union in which he would retain his royal title, but Poppy would not share his rank or privileges. The couple's love prevailed, and they lived a happy, albeit unconventional, life together. Poppy brought joy and lightness to the prince's life, and he cherished her for being his partner and best friend. Though their marriage was not recognized by the royal court, their love remained strong and true.",
